# The sign uncertainty principle

An uncertainty principle describes a trade-off between a function and its Fourier transform. One
such formulation is the Bourgain–Clozel–Kahane uncertainty principle for the last sign change
{Informal.citep bourgain2010principe}[]. For a nice, eventually nonnegative function
$`f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}` let $`r(f)` be the radius of its last sign change. The
Bourgain–Clozel–Kahane constant $`\mathrm{A}_+(d)` is the infimum of
$`\sqrt{r(f) r(\widehat{f})}` over the nonzero functions $`f` such that $`f`, $`\widehat{f}` are
integrable, $`\widehat{f}` is real, $`f` and $`\widehat{f}` are eventually nonnegative, and
$`f(\mathbf{0}) \le 0`, $`\widehat{f}(\mathbf{0}) \le 0`. Its exact value is known only in
dimension $`12`, where Cohn and Gonçalves proved $`\mathrm{A}_+(12) = \sqrt{2}`
{Informal.citep cohn2019optimal}[]. The main theorem of {Informal.citet lee2026sign}[] is the bound

$$`\mathrm{A}_+(d) \le \sqrt{2 \left\lfloor \frac{d}{16} \right\rfloor + 2}`

for every $`d` divisible by $`4`, strict unless $`d = 12`. It is proved with the Fourier
eigenfunctions of Feigenbaum, Grabner and Hardin {Informal.citep feigenbaum2021eigenfunctions}[],
which are Laplace transforms of certain quasimodular forms. Two kinds of positivity are needed. The
first is that the associated quasimodular forms are positive on the imaginary axis; it is
established through extremal quasimodular forms of depth $`2` and hypergeometric identities, and
is not formalized here. The second is that the value of the eigenfunction at the origin has the
right sign. That value turns out to be a positive multiple of a positive combination of the first
few Fourier coefficients of a quasimodular form $`\widetilde{F}_{w-2}` (for the
$`(-1)^{d/4}`-eigenfunctions) or $`\widetilde{G}_{w-12}` (for the
$`(-1)^{d/4+1}`-eigenfunctions), so it comes down to sign conditions on those coefficients, which
are proved by inductions on the weight driven by recurrence relations and modular linear
differential equations. This second kind of positivity is what the formalization covers: the
main purpose of the formalization is to verify the routine but lengthy computations, so that
readers can focus on the main ideas of the proofs.

# Quasimodular forms, twice
%%%
file := "Two-models"
%%%

The ring of quasimodular forms of level $`1` is generated by the Eisenstein series $`E_2`, $`E_4`,
$`E_6`, and closed under the derivative $`D = q \frac{d}{dq}`. Mathlib contains modular forms, the
Eisenstein series $`E_2`, and the derivative and Serre derivative as operators on functions on the
upper half plane. The formalization instead introduces two lighter models, for two reasons. The
family $`\widetilde{F}_{w-2}` is defined with the operator $`\delta = \partial / \partial E_2`,
which is difficult to express for functions on the upper half plane. And all the identities that
are needed are identities of $`q`-expansions, for which formal power series are better suited.

So $`E_2`, $`E_4`, $`E_6` are defined twice. In the _power series model_ they are the formal
power series with the Fourier coefficients of the Eisenstein series, and $`D` and the Serre
derivative $`\partial_k = D - \frac{k}{12} E_2` act on formal power series. In the
_polynomial model_ the ring they generate is the polynomial ring $`\mathbb{R}[E_2, E_4, E_6]`,
which is legitimate because $`E_2`, $`E_4`, $`E_6` are algebraically independent; there $`D` is
the derivation determined by Ramanujan's identities, and $`\delta` and the weight operator make
sense. The $`q`-expansion map from the polynomial model to the power series model connects the two.

# Axioms

Proving Ramanujan's identities from the $`q`-expansions of the Eisenstein series amounts to a
nontrivial convolution identity for divisor sums, which is orthogonal to the operator bookkeeping
the development is about. The three identities are therefore taken as axioms of the power series
model. They are true statements about the series defined in the development, so no inconsistency is
introduced, and they are the only axioms used by the results on the two families beyond Lean's
standard `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`. The coefficient formulas for the
Kaneko–Zagier operators, the calculus of $`D`, $`\delta` and the weight operator in the polynomial
model (but not its comparison with the power series model), and all inequalities other than the
one for $`X_{16,5}` do not use them. The chapter on $`X_{16,5}` uses Deligne's bound for the Ramanujan
$`\tau` function {Informal.citep deligne1974weil}[], again as an axiom.

# Comparator challenges

The main results are restated in the directory `PosqmfChallenge`, one file per topic, each
importing only Mathlib and therefore restating every definition that its statements need.
[Comparator](https://github.com/leanprover/comparator) checks, without trusting the proof files,
that each of these theorems is proved in the library with exactly the stated statement, that the
definitions behind the statements are the same, that no axiom is used beyond the permitted ones,
and that the Lean kernel accepts the proofs. The axioms above are restated in the challenge files
and permitted by name; Comparator then also checks that the library's axioms of those names say
exactly what the challenge files say.
